<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fraumünster</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">853–<a href="/years/1524">1524</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Fraumünster Abbey</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Fraumünster is a church in Zürich that began as a royal abbey for noblewomen, founded in 853 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a> for his daughter Hildegard. Its abbesses held wide rights in Zürich and its lands, including the valley of Uri, and long minted the town's coins. The last abbess handed the abbey to the city in 1524 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fraumunster.jpg" alt="Fraumünster" width="320" height="533" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CEphoto, Uwe Aranas · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Z%C3%BCrich_Switzerland-M%C3%BCnsterbr%C3%BCcke-and-Fraum%C3%BCnster-01_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
